INT. BANKRUPT BAKERY - ARBITRATION - DAY

Empty display cases. Flour dust on the floor. A single steel table in the middle of the shop, two chairs, a stack of papers.

ARBITRATOR VASHTI NAKASHIMA-REID, 50s, grey suit, tablet, sits at the head, scrolling. She looks at her watch.

VASHTI
Ms. Okoro is late.

She puts down the tablet. Rests her hands. The shop is quiet. A ceiling fan turns very slowly.

The bell above the door jangles. GWENDOLYN OKORO, 40s, the baker, apron over a black dress, flour on her shoes, hurries in with a banker's box. She sets it on the table. Her hands shake.

GWENDOLYN
I'm sorry. Traffic.

VASHTI
There is no traffic.

GWENDOLYN
There was a duck.

VASHTI
A duck.

GWENDOLYN
On the road. I stopped.

Vashti stares. Gwendolyn sits. She opens the box and removes a stack of ledgers. Vashti watches her put them in order, one by one, with the care of a woman putting off a conversation.

VASHTI
Shall we begin?

GWENDOLYN
Please.

VASHTI
You are claiming the landlord broke the lease when he padlocked the ovens.

GWENDOLYN
Yes.

VASHTI
He claims you were four months in arrears.

GWENDOLYN
I was three.

VASHTI
The records show four.

GWENDOLYN
The records are wrong.

VASHTI
Can you prove it?

Gwendolyn lays a hand on the top ledger. She slides it across. Vashti opens it, turns the pages, and reads. Her finger traces a column. Stops.

VASHTI (CONT'D)
This is dated March.

GWENDOLYN
Yes.

VASHTI
It's a deposit. Three thousand.

GWENDOLYN
The landlord's own bank stamp.

Vashti sets the ledger down. Takes off her glasses.

VASHTI
Ms. Okoro, why didn't you bring this at the preliminary?

GWENDOLYN
Because I didn't know it was there.

VASHTI
I don't follow.

GWENDOLYN
My husband kept the books. He died in April. I found them last night.

Vashti is quiet. The fan turns. A tear rolls down Gwendolyn's cheek and falls on the ledger. She wipes it with her sleeve, ashamed.

VASHTI
I'm sorry.

GWENDOLYN
It was a stupid, small thing. He never missed a deposit in thirty years.

VASHTI
It's not small.

She gets up. Walks to the glass case. Rests her palm on it, looking at the empty shelves.

VASHTI (CONT'D)
My mother had a bakery. In Osaka. When it closed she sat in the dark for a week.

GWENDOLYN
What did she do after?

VASHTI
She opened another.

Gwendolyn laughs through tears. Vashti returns to the table. Straightens the ledgers, and picks up her pen.

VASHTI (CONT'D)
I'm going to rule in your favour. Then I'm going to ask you what's on the menu.
